  "class_comparisons": [
    {
      "native_id": "MetaCyc:Quinones",
      "native_source": {
        "path": "/private/tmp/pathwaymech-next-255-chemistry/originals-meta27/META-Quinones.xml",
        "bytes": 17892,
        "sha256": "df6ff7e4fbd2b0699369f2fb5e57f08881d97a83037cd6e44f00bef784ba5bc2"
      },
      "direct_chebi_links": [
        "36141"
      ],
      "native_comment": "Quinones are organic compounds derived from aromatic compounds, with two carbonyl groups incorporated into the ring structure with rearrangement of the double bonds where necessary. Quinones are conjugated but not aromatic.\n<p>\nA classic example of a quinone is <a href=\"/compound?orgid=META&id=P-BENZOQUINONE\">1,4-benzoquinone</a>. Quinones are commonly named with a prefix that indicates the parent aromatic hydrocarbon (for example, \"benzo-\" for benzene, \"naphtho-\" for naphthalene, and \"anthra-\" for anthracene) with a \"-quinone\" suffix.\n<p>\nSeveral types of quinones serve as electron acceptors in electron transport chains. Quinones can carry two electrons, and when fully reduced they are referred to as <a href=\"/META/NEW-IMAGE?type=COMPOUND&object=Reduced-Quinones\" title=\"A quinol, or hydroquinone, is the fully reduced form of <a href=''/META/NEW-IMAGE?type=COMPOUND&object=Quinones''>a quinone</a>.&#10;&#10;Quinones are organic compounds derived from aromatic compounds, with two carbonyl groups incorporated into the ring structure with rearrangement of the double bonds where necessary. Quinones are conjugated but not aromatic.&#10;&#10;A classic example of a quinone is <a href=''/compound?orgid=META&id=P-BENZOQUINONE''>1,4-benzoquinone</a>. Quinones are commonly named with a prefix that indicates the parent aromatic hydrocarbon (for example, ''benzo-'' for benzene, ''naphtho-'' for naphthalene, and ''anthra-'' for anthracene) with a ''-quinone'' suffix.&#10;&#10;Several types of quinones serve as electron acceptors in electron transport chains. Quinones can carry two electrons, and when fully reduced they are referred to as <a href=''/META/NEW-IMAGE?type=COMPOUND&object=Reduced-Quinones''>quinols</a>. The addition of a single H atom to a quinone results in formation of a free radical called <a href=''/META/NEW-IMAGE?type=COMPOUND&object=Semiquinones''>a semiquinone</a>.&#10;&#10;Many quinone derivatives often have other biological activities, such as <a href=''/META/NEW-IMAGE?type=COMPOUND&object=CPD-11501''>vitamin K</a>.\">quinols</a>. The addition of a single H atom to a quinone results in formation of a free radical called <a href=\"/META/NEW-IMAGE?type=COMPOUND&object=Semiquinones\" title=\"A semiquinone is a free radical resulting from the removal of one hydrogen atom with its electron during the process of dehydrogenation of <a href=''/META/NEW-IMAGE?type=COMPOUND&object=Reduced-Quinones''>a quinol</a> to <a href=''/META/NEW-IMAGE?type=COMPOUND&object=Quinones''>a quinone</a> or, alternatively, the addition of a single hydrogen atom to <a href=''/META/NEW-IMAGE?type=COMPOUND&object=Quinones''>a quinone</a>.&#10;&#10;Quinones are organic compounds derived from aromatic compounds, with two carbonyl groups incorporated into the ring structure with rearrangement of the double bonds where necessary. Quinones are conjugated but not aromatic.&#10;&#10;A classic example of a quinone is <a href=''/compound?orgid=META&id=P-BENZOQUINONE''>1,4-benzoquinone</a>. Quinones are commonly named with a prefix that indicates the parent aromatic hydrocarbon (for example, ''benzo-'' for benzene, ''naphtho-'' for naphthalene, and ''anthra-'' for anthracene) with a ''-quinone'' suffix.&#10;&#10;Several types of quinones serve as electron acceptors in electron transport chains. Quinones can carry two electrons, and when fully reduced they are referred to as <a href=''/META/NEW-IMAGE?type=COMPOUND&object=Reduced-Quinones''>quinols</a>. The addition of a single H atom to a quinone results in formation of a free radical called <a href=''/META/NEW-IMAGE?type=COMPOUND&object=Semiquinones''>a semiquinone</a>.&#10;&#10;Many quinone derivatives often have other biological activities, such as <a href=''/META/NEW-IMAGE?type=COMPOUND&object=CPD-11501''>vitamin K</a>.\">a semiquinone</a>.\n<p>\nMany quinone derivatives often have other biological activities, such as <a href=\"/META/NEW-IMAGE?type=COMPOUND&object=CPD-11501\" title=\"Vitamin K is a name given to a group of derivatives  of 2-methyl-1,4-naphthoquinone that are required by mammals for the posttranslational modification of certain proteins, mostly involved in blood coagulation.&#10;These compounds, while essential for mammals, are not synthesized by them, and are thus considered vitamins.&#10;&#10;The natural forms of the vitamin are vitamin K<sub>1</sub> (<a href=''/compound?orgid=META&id=2-METHYL-3-PHYTYL-14-NAPHTHOQUINONE''>phylloquinone</a>) and vitamin K<sub>2</sub> (<a href=''/META/NEW-IMAGE?type=COMPOUND&object=Menaquinones''>a menaquinone</a>). Since menaquinones produced by different organisms have different tail lenghts, there are many variations of vitamin K<sub>2</sub>, usually specified based on the number of carbons in their tails, such as in Vitamin K<sub>2</sub>(45).&#10;Menaquinones are synthesized by gut bacteria, and are absorbed into the blood. Thus, dietary deficiency is extremely rare in healthy individuals.&#10;&#10;There are also several synthetic forms-   vitamin K<sub>3</sub> through K<sub>9</sub>. Vitamin K<sub>3</sub> (<a href=''/compound?orgid=META&id=CPD-3766''>menadione</a>) is the most active of the synthetic forms.&#10;\">vitamin K</a>.",
      "native_representative_smiles": "C1([R4])(=C([R3])C(=O)C(/[R3])=C([R1])/C(=O)1)",
